---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import Card from '../../components/ui/Card.astro';
import CardContent from '../../components/ui/CardContent.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';

const headings = [
  { text: "Overview", slug: "overview", depth: 2 },
  { text: "What It Can and Cannot See", slug: "limits", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Requirements", slug: "requirements", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Install the Agent", slug: "install", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Docker Compose", slug: "install-compose", depth: 3 },
  { text: "Standalone Docker", slug: "install-docker", depth: 3 },
  { text: "systemd", slug: "install-systemd", depth: 3 },
  { text: "Verify", slug: "verify", depth: 3 },
  { text: "Configuration", slug: "configuration", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Permissions", slug: "permissions", depth: 2 },
  { text: "What Is Collected", slug: "metrics", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Health Rules", slug: "health-rules", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Hardware Events", slug: "events", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Hardware Health Detection Pack", slug: "detection-pack", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Notifications and Incidents", slug: "notifications", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Troubleshooting", slug: "troubleshooting", depth: 2 }
];

const keywords = [
  "hardware health monitoring",
  "SMART disk monitoring",
  "ECC memory errors monitoring",
  "IPMI sensor monitoring",
  "homelab hardware monitoring",
  "self-hosted hardware monitoring",
  "smartmontools alerting"
];

const th = "py-2 pr-4 font-semibold";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Hardware Health"
  description="Early warning for failing disks, RAM, fans and power on bare-metal servers and homelabs: the LogTide hardware agent, health rules, hardware events and notifications."
  keywords={keywords}
  headings={headings}
>
  <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
    <div class="p-2 bg-primary/10 rounded-lg">
      <Icon name="lucide:cpu" class="w-6 h-6 text-primary-readable" />
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold">Hardware Health</h1>
  </div>

  <p class="text-lg text-muted-foreground mb-8">
    Watch the physical health of your servers from <strong>Monitor → Hardware</strong>: SMART data
    from disks, ECC memory errors, temperatures, fans, power supplies and network cards, plus
    hardware events from the kernel log and the BMC. Available since LogTide 1.7.0, open source.
  </p>

  <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-8">
    Prefer a walkthrough? Follow the step-by-step tutorial
    <a href="/blog/monitor-home-server-hardware-health/" class="text-primary-readable underline hover:text-primary-readable/80">Monitor a home server's disks, RAM and fans with LogTide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="overview" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Overview
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <p>
      Hardware Health is early warning, not prediction. It reads the signals that hardware gives
      out before it fails (reallocated sectors, corrected memory errors, a fan slowing down, a
      PCIe link retraining) and turns them into a status per host and per component, with an
      incident and a notification when something gets worse.
    </p>

    <p>A small agent runs on each server and sends two things to your LogTide project:</p>

    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 ml-2">
      <li><strong>Metrics</strong> named <code>hw.*</code>, sent as OTLP gauges to <code>POST /v1/otlp/metrics</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Events</strong> from the kernel log and the BMC event log, sent as logs with service <code>hardware</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      LogTide registers each host the first time it sees <code>hw.*</code> metrics from it,
      evaluates it every two minutes and shows the result on <strong>Monitor → Hardware</strong>.
      Nothing else is needed: the agent uses the same project API key as your logs.
    </p>

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
      <Card>
        <CardContent class="pt-4">
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <Icon name="lucide:hard-drive" class="w-5 h-5 text-primary-readable mt-0.5 shrink-0" />
            <div>
              <h4 class="font-medium mb-1">Disks</h4>
              <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">HDD, SATA SSD and NVMe: SMART status and attributes, NVMe spare and media errors, wear, temperature.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </CardContent>
      </Card>

      <Card>
        <CardContent class="pt-4">
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <Icon name="lucide:memory-stick" class="w-5 h-5 text-primary-readable mt-0.5 shrink-0" />
            <div>
              <h4 class="font-medium mb-1">Memory</h4>
              <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Corrected and uncorrected ECC errors per memory controller, read from the kernel EDAC subsystem.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </CardContent>
      </Card>

      <Card>
        <CardContent class="pt-4">
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <Icon name="lucide:thermometer" class="w-5 h-5 text-primary-readable mt-0.5 shrink-0" />
            <div>
              <h4 class="font-medium mb-1">CPU and cooling</h4>
              <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">CPU temperature against its limits, thermal throttling, and fan speeds from the motherboard or the BMC.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </CardContent>
      </Card>

      <Card>
        <CardContent class="pt-4">
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <Icon name="lucide:plug-zap" class="w-5 h-5 text-primary-readable mt-0.5 shrink-0" />
            <div>
              <h4 class="font-medium mb-1">Power and network</h4>
              <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">PSU status and voltages from the BMC, link flaps and error counters on physical network cards.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </CardContent>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="limits" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    What It Can and Cannot See
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <div class="bg-yellow-500/10 border border-yellow-500/20 rounded-lg p-4">
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <Icon name="lucide:alert-triangle" class="w-5 h-5 text-yellow-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-semibold text-yellow-600 dark:text-yellow-400 mb-1">Keep your backups</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            SMART does not catch every failing disk. Studies of large disk fleets (Google, Backblaze)
            found that roughly a third of drives that fail show no SMART warning beforehand. A green
            status here means no known warning signs, not that the hardware is guaranteed healthy.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 ml-2">
      <li>
        <strong>Bare metal, homelab and on-premises servers</strong> are the target. Disks, board
        sensors, EDAC and a BMC are all visible there.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Virtual machines and most cloud instances expose no hardware sensors.</strong> The
        agent detects virtualization and reports it, and the host page says that sensors are not
        available instead of showing an empty page. Run the agent on the hypervisor host, not inside
        the guests.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A host that goes completely dark shows as stale.</strong> If no <code>hw.*</code> data
        point arrives for more than 10 minutes, the host is marked <strong>stale</strong>. A stale host
        is shown but does not open an incident. To be told when a machine dies or loses power, pair
        it with a <strong>heartbeat</strong> monitor from Uptime that the server pings on a schedule.
      </li>
      <li>
        Not covered in this release: RAID controller tools (MegaCLI, storcli), UPS (NUT), Redfish,
        per-host custom thresholds, and Windows or macOS hosts.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="requirements" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Requirements
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 ml-2">
      <li>LogTide 1.7.0 or later (self-hosted or Cloud) and a project API key.</li>
      <li>A <strong>Linux</strong> host. The agent is a single Python 3 script with no dependencies.</li>
      <li><code>smartmontools</code> for disk data. Included in the Docker image.</li>
      <li><code>ipmitool</code> and a <strong>BMC</strong> (iDRAC, iLO, IPMI) for PSU, voltage and sensor data from the board. Optional, included in the Docker image.</li>
      <li>The <strong>EDAC</strong> kernel modules loaded for ECC memory counters (<code>/sys/devices/system/edac/mc</code> must exist). Needs ECC RAM and a supporting platform.</li>
      <li><strong>Root or a privileged container</strong>, because SMART, IPMI and <code>/dev/kmsg</code> need it. See <a href="#permissions">Permissions</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      Each collector is independent. A host without a BMC still reports disks, memory and
      temperatures; the missing collector is listed as unavailable on the host page.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="install" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Install the Agent
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-6">
    <p>
      Create a project API key first (<strong>Project settings → API keys</strong>). Use a
      dedicated key for hardware agents so you can rotate it separately. The examples use the
      LogTide Cloud API URL; for a self-hosted instance set <code>LOGTIDE_URL</code> to your own
      API base URL.
    </p>

    <div>
      <h3 id="install-compose" class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3 scroll-mt-24">Docker Compose</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-3">
        If the host that runs LogTide is the machine you want to monitor, enable the
        <code>hardware</code> profile of the LogTide compose file. Put the key in
        <code>.env</code> and start the profile:
      </p>
      <Code
        lang="bash"
        theme="one-dark-pro"
        code={`# docker/.env
HW_AGENT_API_KEY=lp_your_ingestion_key_here

docker compose --profile hardware up -d`}
      />
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mt-3">
        If <code>HW_AGENT_API_KEY</code> is empty, <code>FLUENT_BIT_API_KEY</code> is used. With
        neither set, every request is rejected with 401 while the container still looks healthy.
        The service is called <code>hw-agent</code>, runs privileged, and talks to the backend over
        the compose network. Use <code>HW_AGENT_IMAGE</code> to pin a different image.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h3 id="install-docker" class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3 scroll-mt-24">Standalone Docker</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-3">
        For every other server, run the agent as a single container and point it at your LogTide
        API with your own URL and key:
      </p>
      <Code
        lang="bash"
        theme="one-dark-pro"
        code={`docker run -d --name logtide-hw-agent --restart unless-stopped --privileged \\
  -e LOGTIDE_URL=https://api.logtide.dev \\
  -e LOGTIDE_API_KEY=lp_your_key \\
  -e HOST_ROOT=/host \\
  -v /sys:/host/sys:ro \\
  -v /proc:/host/proc:ro \\
  -v /etc/hostname:/host/etc/hostname:ro \\
  -v logtide-hw-agent-state:/var/lib/logtide-hw-agent \\
  logtide/hw-agent:latest`}
      />
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mt-3">
        The mounts and <code>--privileged</code> are explained under <a href="#permissions">Permissions</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h3 id="install-systemd" class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3 scroll-mt-24">systemd</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-3">
        Without Docker, run the single-file agent as a systemd service. The example is for
        Debian and Ubuntu; install <code>ipmitool</code> only on servers with a BMC.
      </p>
      <Code
        lang="bash"
        theme="one-dark-pro"
        code={`sudo apt install -y python3 smartmontools ipmitool
sudo install -d /opt/logtide-hw-agent /etc/logtide
sudo curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/logtide-dev/logtide/main/hw-agent/logtide_hw_agent.py \\
  -o /opt/logtide-hw-agent/logtide_hw_agent.py
sudo curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/logtide-dev/logtide/main/hw-agent/logtide-hw-agent.service \\
  -o /etc/systemd/system/logtide-hw-agent.service
sudo tee /etc/logtide/hw-agent.env >/dev/null <<'ENV'
LOGTIDE_URL=https://api.logtide.dev
LOGTIDE_API_KEY=lp_your_key
ENV
sudo chmod 600 /etc/logtide/hw-agent.env
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable --now logtide-hw-agent`}
      />
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mt-3">
        The service runs as root and keeps its state in
        <code>/var/lib/logtide-hw-agent</code>. Read its log with
        <code>journalctl -u logtide-hw-agent -f</code>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h3 id="verify" class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3 scroll-mt-24">Verify</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-3">
        Before leaving the agent running, do a dry run. It collects once, prints the payloads and
        sends nothing, so you can see which collectors work on this machine:
      </p>
      <Code
        lang="bash"
        theme="one-dark-pro"
        code={`# Docker
docker run --rm --privileged -e HOST_ROOT=/host -e LOGTIDE_HW_DRY_RUN=1 \\
  -v /sys:/host/sys:ro -v /proc:/host/proc:ro -v /etc/hostname:/host/etc/hostname:ro \\
  logtide/hw-agent:latest --once

# systemd
sudo sh -c 'set -a; . /etc/logtide/hw-agent.env; LOGTIDE_HW_DRY_RUN=1 python3 /opt/logtide-hw-agent/logtide_hw_agent.py --once'`}
      />
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mt-3">
        Without <code>LOGTIDE_HW_DRY_RUN</code>, <code>--once</code> collects, sends one real batch
        and exits with code 1 if delivery failed. That is the quickest way to test the URL and the
        key. Then open <strong>Monitor → Hardware</strong>: the host appears within a minute or two.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="configuration" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Configuration
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <p>The agent is configured with environment variables.</p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-border text-left">
            <th class={th}>Variable</th>
            <th class={th}>Default</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Description</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-muted-foreground">
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>LOGTIDE_URL</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">required</td><td class="py-2">Base URL of the LogTide API, for example <code>http://backend:8080</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>LOGTIDE_API_KEY</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">required</td><td class="py-2">Project API key, sent as <code>X-API-Key</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>LOGTIDE_HOSTNAME</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>$HOST_ROOT/etc/hostname</code>, else the system hostname</td><td class="py-2">Value of <code>host.name</code>, the name shown in Monitor → Hardware.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>HOST_ROOT</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">empty</td><td class="py-2">Prefix for <code>/sys</code>, <code>/proc</code> and <code>/etc</code> reads. Set to <code>/host</code> in the container.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>LOGTIDE_HW_INTERVAL</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>60</code></td><td class="py-2">Seconds between collections (minimum 5).</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>LOGTIDE_HW_COLLECTORS</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">all</td><td class="py-2">Comma-separated list of <code>smart,edac,hwmon,thermal,ipmi,sel,net,kmsg</code>. Use it to switch collectors off.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>LOGTIDE_HW_STATE_DIR</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>/var/lib/logtide-hw-agent</code></td><td class="py-2">Where the last BMC event id is stored.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>LOGTIDE_HW_DRY_RUN</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">off</td><td class="py-2"><code>1</code> prints payloads to stdout instead of sending them.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>LOGTIDE_HW_KMSG_PATH</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>/dev/kmsg</code></td><td class="py-2">Kernel log device. Only useful for testing.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      The command line flag <code>--once</code> runs one collection, sends it and exits. In the
      compose stack the agent reads <code>HW_AGENT_API_KEY</code> and <code>HW_AGENT_IMAGE</code>
      from <code>.env</code>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="permissions" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Permissions
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <p>
      The agent runs as root because SMART queries, <code>/dev/kmsg</code> and
      <code>/dev/ipmi0</code> need raw device access. It only reads: it does not write to disks,
      change fan settings or modify the BMC. In the container, the setup is:
    </p>

    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 ml-2">
      <li>
        <code>/sys</code> and <code>/proc</code> are mounted read-only under <code>/host</code> and
        read through <code>HOST_ROOT</code>. The container's own <code>/sys/class/net</code> only
        shows the container's network namespace, so NIC counters would be wrong without the host mount.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--privileged</code> gives the container the host's <code>/dev</code> (SATA and NVMe
        disks, <code>/dev/kmsg</code>, <code>/dev/ipmi0</code>) and the capabilities
        <code>smartctl</code> needs.
      </li>
      <li>
        The <code>/etc/hostname</code> mount makes the host name match the real host instead of the
        container id. You can set <code>LOGTIDE_HOSTNAME</code> instead.
      </li>
      <li>The state volume keeps the BMC event log position across restarts, so old events are not sent again.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      Without <code>--privileged</code> you need <code>--cap-add SYS_RAWIO --cap-add SYS_ADMIN
      --cap-add SYSLOG</code> plus one <code>--device</code> per disk (<code>/dev/sda</code>,
      <code>/dev/nvme0</code>), <code>--device /dev/kmsg</code>, and <code>--device /dev/ipmi0</code>
      if present. This is more fragile, because seccomp and AppArmor profiles vary between
      distributions, and privileged is the tested path.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="metrics" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    What Is Collected
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <p>
      Every data point carries the attributes <code>host.name</code>, <code>hw.type</code> and
      <code>hw.id</code> (the device, sensor or controller it belongs to). All metrics are gauges.
      Counters such as error totals are sent as the raw cumulative value, and LogTide works on the
      growth over a window, so a reboot that resets a counter does not look like an improvement or
      a spike.
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-border text-left">
            <th class={th}>Metric</th>
            <th class={th}>Unit</th>
            <th class={th}>Extra attributes</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Source</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-muted-foreground">
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.host.info</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.virtual</code>, <code>hw.vendor</code>, <code>hw.model</code>, <code>os.kernel</code>, <code>agent.version</code></td><td class="py-2">DMI, <code>/proc</code></td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.agent.collector.status</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">1 / 0</td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>collector</code>: smart, edac, hwmon, ipmi, net, thermal, kmsg, sel</td><td class="py-2">Agent</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.physical_disk.smart_status</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">1 ok / 0 failed</td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>device</code>, <code>model</code>, <code>serial</code>, <code>protocol</code> (ata, nvme, scsi)</td><td class="py-2">smartctl</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.physical_disk.smart</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">raw value</td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>smart_attribute</code>: reallocated_sector_count, current_pending_sector, offline_uncorrectable, reported_uncorrect, udma_crc_error_count, command_timeout, reallocated_event_count, power_on_hours</td><td class="py-2">smartctl (ATA)</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.physical_disk.endurance_utilization</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">%</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">-</td><td class="py-2">NVMe percentage used, ATA wear attributes</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.physical_disk.available_spare</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">%</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">-</td><td class="py-2">NVMe</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.physical_disk.available_spare_threshold</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">%</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">-</td><td class="py-2">NVMe</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.physical_disk.media_errors</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">count</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">-</td><td class="py-2">NVMe</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.physical_disk.critical_warning</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">bitmask</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">-</td><td class="py-2">NVMe</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.temperature</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">Cel</td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>sensor</code>; <code>hw.type</code> is cpu, physical_disk, memory, board, gpu or other</td><td class="py-2">hwmon, smartctl, ipmitool</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.temperature.limit</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">Cel</td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>sensor</code>, <code>limit_type</code> (high, critical)</td><td class="py-2">hwmon, ipmitool</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.fan.speed</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">rpm</td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>sensor</code></td><td class="py-2">hwmon, ipmitool</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.fan.speed.limit</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">rpm</td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>limit_type</code> (low.critical)</td><td class="py-2">ipmitool</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.voltage</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">V</td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>sensor</code></td><td class="py-2">hwmon, ipmitool</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.voltage.limit</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">V</td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>limit_type</code> (low.critical, high.critical)</td><td class="py-2">hwmon, ipmitool</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.power_supply.status</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">1 / 0</td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>sensor</code></td><td class="py-2">ipmitool</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.memory.errors</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">count (cumulative)</td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>error_type</code> (corrected, uncorrected); <code>hw.id</code> is the memory controller or DIMM label</td><td class="py-2">EDAC</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.memory.size</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">By</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">-</td><td class="py-2"><code>/proc/meminfo</code></td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.cpu.throttle_count</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">count (cumulative)</td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>throttle_type</code> (core, package)</td><td class="py-2">sysfs thermal_throttle</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.network.errors</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">count (cumulative)</td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>direction</code> (receive, transmit), <code>error_type</code> (errors, crc, dropped)</td><td class="py-2">sysfs, physical NICs only</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.network.carrier_changes</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">count (cumulative)</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">-</td><td class="py-2">sysfs</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>hw.network.up</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">1 / 0</td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>speed_mbps</code></td><td class="py-2">sysfs</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      The metrics are ordinary OTLP metrics, so you can also chart them in the Metrics Explorer or
      with a <code>metric_chart</code> panel on a <a href="/docs/dashboards/">custom dashboard</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="health-rules" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Health Rules
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <p>
      Each host has six components: <strong>Disks</strong>, <strong>Memory</strong>,
      <strong>CPU</strong>, <strong>Cooling</strong>, <strong>Power</strong> and
      <strong>Network</strong>. A component is <code>ok</code>, <code>warning</code>,
      <code>critical</code> or <code>unknown</code> (no data). The host status is the worst of its
      components. Every finding on the host page names the component, the device and the value that
      crossed the threshold.
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-border text-left">
            <th class={th}>Component</th>
            <th class={th}>Warning</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Critical</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-muted-foreground">
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Disks</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">Any reallocated sector, pending sector or reported uncorrectable error. NVMe media errors grew in 7 days. UDMA CRC errors grew in 24 hours (usually a bad cable). Endurance used at 80% or more. Temperature at 55 C or more for HDD and SATA SSD, 70 C or more for NVMe.</td><td class="py-2">SMART overall status failed. Offline uncorrectable sectors above 0. NVMe critical warning set. NVMe available spare below its threshold. Endurance used at 95% or more. Reallocated sectors grew by 10 or more in 7 days. Temperature at 65 C or more for HDD and SATA SSD, 80 C or more for NVMe.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Memory</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">Corrected ECC errors grew in the last 24 hours.</td><td class="py-2">Any uncorrected ECC error since boot or in the last 7 days. Corrected errors grew by 100 or more in 24 hours (a DIMM degrading fast).</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">CPU</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">Temperature at or above the sensor's high limit. Thermal throttle count grew in 24 hours.</td><td class="py-2">Temperature at or above the sensor's critical limit. When a sensor reports no limits, 85 C and 95 C are used.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Cooling</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">-</td><td class="py-2">A fan below its low-critical limit, or at 0 rpm after reporting a higher speed in the last 24 hours.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Power</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">A voltage outside its limits.</td><td class="py-2">A power supply reporting failure.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4">Network</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">CRC errors grew in 24 hours. 5 or more carrier changes (link flaps) in 24 hours. 100 or more errors in 24 hours.</td><td class="py-2">-</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 ml-2">
      <li>Only physical network interfaces are evaluated. Bridges, VLANs, VPNs and container interfaces are ignored.</li>
      <li>Rules that look at growth need history. Right after you install the agent, growth-based findings can take up to 24 hours (or 7 days for the 7-day rules) to become meaningful.</li>
      <li>The thresholds are fixed in this release and shared by the backend and the UI. Per-host custom thresholds are not available yet.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="events" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Hardware Events
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <p>Besides metrics, the agent forwards hardware events as logs:</p>

    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 ml-2">
      <li>
        <strong>Kernel log.</strong> The agent tails <code>/dev/kmsg</code> from the moment it
        starts (no replay of old messages) and keeps only lines that look like hardware problems:
        disk I/O errors, ATA link resets, EDAC and machine check messages, PCIe AER errors, thermal
        throttling, NIC link changes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>BMC System Event Log.</strong> New entries from <code>ipmitool sel elist</code> are
        forwarded once. The agent remembers the last entry it sent in its state directory.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Each event is a log with service <code>hardware</code>, the host name as <code>hostname</code>,
      and metadata <code>hw.event.source</code> (<code>kmsg</code> or <code>sel</code>) and
      <code>hw.event.type</code> (<code>disk</code>, <code>memory</code>, <code>cpu</code>,
      <code>pcie</code>, <code>power</code>, <code>thermal</code>, <code>network</code> or
      <code>other</code>). The level follows the kernel priority; SEL entries are warnings, and
      critical when they mention an uncorrectable error, a failure or a predictive failure. Recent
      events for a host appear on its page, and you can search them in the log explorer by
      service <code>hardware</code>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="detection-pack" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Hardware Health Detection Pack
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <p>
      The <strong>Hardware Health</strong> detection pack is a bundle of Sigma rules, in a new
      <code>hardware</code> pack category, that match hardware problems in kernel and syslog
      messages. Enable it from the detection packs page like any other pack.
    </p>

    <p>
      The rules match on the message text, so they fire on agent events and on plain kernel lines
      that reach LogTide some other way. If you already ship kernel logs through
      <a href="/docs/syslog/">syslog</a>, journald or Fluent Bit, the pack works <strong>without the
      agent</strong>; you just do not get the metrics and the host page.
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-border text-left">
            <th class={th}>Area</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Matches messages such as</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-muted-foreground">
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Memory</td><td class="py-2">EDAC corrected and uncorrected errors, <code>Hardware Error</code>, <code>Machine check events logged</code></td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Disk</td><td class="py-2"><code>I/O error, dev</code>, <code>hard resetting link</code>, <code>SATA link down</code>, <code>failed command: READ FPDMA</code>, <code>Medium Error</code>, <code>Buffer I/O error</code>, NVMe timeouts and <code>controller is down</code></td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">PCIe</td><td class="py-2"><code>AER: Corrected error</code>, <code>AER: Uncorrected</code>, <code>PCIe Bus Error</code></td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Thermal</td><td class="py-2"><code>temperature above threshold</code>, <code>cpu clock throttled</code></td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-border/50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Power</td><td class="py-2"><code>Power supply</code> and <code>redundancy lost</code> events from the BMC log</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4">Network</td><td class="py-2"><code>NIC Link is Down</code>, <code>Detected Tx Unit Hang</code>, <code>link is not ready</code> (low severity)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      For general Sigma usage see the
      <a href="/use-cases/security-monitoring/" class="text-primary-readable underline hover:text-primary-readable/80">security monitoring guide</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="notifications" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Notifications and Incidents
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 ml-2">
      <li>
        When a host gets worse (to <code>warning</code> or <code>critical</code>), LogTide opens or
        updates an incident with source <code>hardware</code> in the
        <a href="/docs/incidents/">incidents inbox</a>. A warning maps to medium severity, a critical to critical.
      </li>
      <li>
        The notification uses the new <strong>Hardware health</strong> event type. Choose its
        destinations (email, Slack, Discord, Teams, webhook) under
        <strong>Settings → Notifications → Routing</strong>, as described in
        <a href="/docs/notification-routing/">Notification Routing</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        When the host returns to <code>ok</code>, the incident is resolved and a resolved
        notification is sent.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Mute a host</strong> on its detail page while you work on it. A muted host is still
        evaluated and shown, but opens no incidents and sends no notifications. Forgetting a host
        removes it from the list; it comes back by itself if the agent keeps sending data.
      </li>
      <li>A stale host does not open an incident. Use a heartbeat monitor for that, see <a href="#limits">above</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="troubleshooting" class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4 scroll-mt-24 border-b border-border pb-2">
    Troubleshooting
  </h2>

  <div class="mb-8 space-y-6">
    <div>
      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2 scroll-mt-24">No hosts appear</h3>
      <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 ml-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
        <li>Run the agent once in the foreground (see <a href="#verify">Verify</a>) and read its output. It reports HTTP errors from LogTide.</li>
        <li>Check the URL and key. A 401 means the API key is wrong. The URL is the base URL of your instance, not a path.</li>
        <li>Make sure the key belongs to the project you are looking at in the project switcher.</li>
        <li>Allow a minute or two. A host is registered on the first batch and evaluated every two minutes.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2 scroll-mt-24">A collector shows as unavailable</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
        The host page lists every collector with its state. Unavailable means the agent could not
        run it: <code>smart</code> needs <code>smartctl</code> and privileges to open the devices,
        <code>ipmi</code> and <code>sel</code> need <code>ipmitool</code> and a BMC device,
        <code>edac</code> needs ECC memory and the EDAC modules, <code>kmsg</code> needs read access
        to <code>/dev/kmsg</code>. On hardware without a BMC or without ECC, unavailable is expected.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2 scroll-mt-24">The host is detected as a virtual machine</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
        The agent reads DMI data and reports <code>hw.virtual</code>. Guests see virtual disks and
        no sensors, so the page shows a banner instead of component data. Install the agent on the
        hypervisor host instead.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2 scroll-mt-24">NVMe temperatures are missing</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
        NVMe temperatures come from SMART and from the kernel hwmon interface. If they are missing,
        check that <code>smartctl -a /dev/nvme0</code> works as root on the host and that your
        <code>smartmontools</code> is recent enough to support <code>-j</code> JSON output
        (7.0 or newer).
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2 scroll-mt-24">IPMI permission errors</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
        <code>ipmitool</code> talks to the BMC through <code>/dev/ipmi0</code>, which only root can
        open, and the <code>ipmi_devintf</code> and <code>ipmi_si</code> kernel modules must be
        loaded. Run <code>sudo ipmitool sensor</code> on the host: if that fails, the agent will fail
        too. In a container, make sure the device is passed through.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-2 scroll-mt-24">A host turned stale</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
        No <code>hw.*</code> data for 10 minutes. Check that the agent is running and that the
        host and LogTide can still reach each other. Stale hosts do not notify; add a heartbeat
        monitor if you need to know about it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="border-t border-border pt-6 mt-8">
    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      Related: <a href="/docs/syslog/" class="text-primary-readable hover:underline">Syslog integration</a>,
      <a href="/docs/notification-routing/" class="text-primary-readable hover:underline">Notification Routing</a>,
      <a href="/docs/incidents/" class="text-primary-readable hover:underline">Incidents Inbox</a>,
      <a href="/blog/monitor-home-server-hardware-health/" class="text-primary-readable hover:underline">Tutorial: monitor a home server</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
